    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 102,
      "topLeftY": 151,
      "bottomRightX": 613,
      "bottomRightY": 233,
      "content": "We shall now obtain the wave equation as a differential equation in r, with the variables that specify the orientation of the whole system removed. We can do this by the use only of elementary non-commutative algebra in the following way.",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 117,
      "topLeftY": 239,
      "bottomRightX": 420,
      "bottomRightY": 259,
      "content": "In formula (16) take B = C = m. This gives",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 194,
      "topLeftY": 266,
      "bottomRightX": 610,
      "bottomRightY": 334,
      "content": "$$\\begin{array}{l} (\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m})^2 = \\mathbf{m}^2 + i(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m} \\times \\mathbf{m}) \\\\ = (\\mathbf{m} + \\frac{1}{2}h\\boldsymbol{\\sigma})^2 - h(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) - \\frac{1}{4}h^2\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}^2 - h(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) \\\\ = \\mathbf{M}^2 - 2h(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) - \\frac{3}{4}h^2. \\end{array} \\tag{20}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 104,
      "topLeftY": 332,
      "bottomRightX": 148,
      "bottomRightY": 347,
      "content": "Hence",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 155,
      "topLeftY": 347,
      "bottomRightX": 376,
      "bottomRightY": 368,
      "content": "$$\\{(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) + h\\}^2 = \\mathbf{M}^2 + \\frac{1}{4}h^2 = j^2h^2.$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 103,
      "topLeftY": 373,
      "bottomRightX": 614,
      "bottomRightY": 456,
      "content": "Up to the present we have defined j only through j², so that we could now, if we liked, take jh equal to (σ, m) + h. This would not be convenient since we want j to be a constant of the motion while (σ, m) + h is not, although its square is. We have, in fact, by another application of (16),",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 252,
      "topLeftY": 463,
      "bottomRightX": 464,
      "bottomRightY": 481,
      "content": "$$(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m})(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{p}) = i(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m} \\times \\mathbf{p})$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 103,
      "topLeftY": 487,
      "bottomRightX": 312,
      "bottomRightY": 506,
      "content": "since (m, p) = 0, and similarly",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 250,
      "topLeftY": 512,
      "bottomRightX": 467,
      "bottomRightY": 531,
      "content": "$$(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{p})(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) = i(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{p} \\times \\mathbf{m}),$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 103,
      "topLeftY": 529,
      "bottomRightX": 157,
      "bottomRightY": 544,
      "content": "so that",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 127,
      "topLeftY": 554,
      "bottomRightX": 592,
      "bottomRightY": 597,
      "content": "$$\\begin{array}{l} (\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m})(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{p}) + (\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{p})(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) = i\\Sigma\\sigma_1(m_2p_3 - m_3p_2 + p_2m_3 - p_3m_2) \\\\ = i\\Sigma\\sigma_1 \\cdot 2ihp_1 = -2h(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{p}), \\end{array}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 104,
      "topLeftY": 598,
      "bottomRightX": 122,
      "bottomRightY": 610,
      "content": "or",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 194,
      "topLeftY": 610,
      "bottomRightX": 522,
      "bottomRightY": 631,
      "content": "$$\\{(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) + h\\}(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{p}) + (\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{p})\\{(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) + h\\} = 0.$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 103,
      "topLeftY": 636,
      "bottomRightX": 613,
      "bottomRightY": 717,
      "content": "Thus (σ, m) + h anticommutes with one of the terms in F, namely, ρ₁(σ, p), and commutes with the other three. Hence ρ₃{(σ, m) + h} commutes with all four, and is therefore a constant of the motion. But the square of ρ₃{(σ, m) + h} must also equal j²h². We therefore take",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "text"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 276,
      "topLeftY": 725,
      "bottomRightX": 610,
      "bottomRightY": 744,
      "content": "$$jh = \\rho_3\\{(\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}, \\mathbf{m}) + h\\}. \\tag{21}$$",
      "confidenceScores": null,
      "type": "equation"
    },
    {
      "topLeftX": 118,
      "topLeftY": 749,
      "bottomRightX": 385,
      "bottomRightY": 768,
      "content": "We have, by a further application of (16)",
